/*!
    \page creator-how-to-ask-ai.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-ai

    \title Ask AI for help

    Several places in \QC can hand a task to an AI:

    \list
        \li Explain an issue in \l Issues.
        \li Write a commit message in the \l {git commit}{Git Commit} view.
        \li Send the output of an \l {Use external tools}{external tool} to
            an AI.
    \endlist

    \section1 Select an AI

    When you start such a task, a dialog lists the AIs that can do it:

    \list
        \li The agents that you configured in the \l {Use the ACP Client}
            {ACP Client}.
        \li The custom language models that you added in \preferences >
            \uicontrol AI > \uicontrol {Custom Language Models}.
        \li \uicontrol {Copy to Clipboard}, which copies the task as text, so
            that you can paste it into an AI that \QC does not know.
    \endlist

    \image {qtcreator-ai-target-picker.webp} {Dialog for selecting the AI that does a task}

    Select an AI, set its options, such as the model or how much effort it
    puts into the task, and then select \uicontrol Send. To send the task with
    the options as they are, double-click the AI.

    An agent offers the options that it reported in its last chat, so start a
    chat with an agent once to see its options here.

    \QC remembers your choice for each kind of task, and selects it again the
    next time.

    \section1 What the AI does with the task

    What happens next depends on the AI that you selected:

    \table
    \header
        \li AI
        \li Result
    \row
        \li Agent
        \li A new chat opens in the side panel with the task as its first
            message. You can continue the chat as usual.

            If the chat asks you to accept terms and conditions first, accept
            them and start the task again.
    \row
        \li Custom language model
        \li \QC runs the command of the model with the task as input, and shows
            the answer in \l {View output}{General Messages}.
    \row
        \li \uicontrol {Copy to Clipboard}
        \li The task and its context are on the clipboard as text.
    \endtable

    A task usually comes with context, such as the lines of code around an
    issue or the output of a tool. In the chat, the context appears as chips
    above the message. Select a file chip to open the file, and a text chip to
    view the text in an editor.

    \image {qtcreator-acp-chat-context.webp} {Context of a task as chips in the chat}

    \section1 Tasks that return an answer

    Some tasks, such as writing a commit message, need the answer back in \QC.
    The answer goes into the task, such as the commit message, instead of a
    chat or \uicontrol {General Messages}. Only agents and custom language
    models can do these tasks, so the dialog does not offer
    \uicontrol {Copy to Clipboard} for them.

    An agent does such a task without a chat. It cannot ask you for
    permission to run a tool or for other input, so \QC denies these requests.
    If the agent requires you to sign in, sign in from a chat with the agent
    first.

    \sa {Use the ACP Client}, {Issues}, {git commit}, {Use external tools}
*/
